1
00:00:00,160 --> 00:00:02,240
Your coffee doesn't cool at a steady

2
00:00:02,240 --> 00:00:04,240
speed. It cools fastest while it's

3
00:00:04,240 --> 00:00:06,560
hottest, then slower and slower.

4
00:00:06,560 --> 00:00:08,240
A simple model can estimate when it

5
00:00:08,240 --> 00:00:10,160
reaches the temperature you like, and

6
00:00:10,160 --> 00:00:12,480
whether to add the milk now or later.

7
00:00:12,480 --> 00:00:14,640
Let's work it out with one law of cooling

8
00:00:14,640 --> 00:00:16,720
and a little exponential math.

9
00:00:16,720 --> 00:00:18,080
Here's our setup.

10
00:00:18,080 --> 00:00:20,000
Fresh coffee at eighty five degrees

11
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:22,320
Celsius, in a room that stays at twenty

12
00:00:22,320 --> 00:00:24,240
degrees. What matters isn't the

13
00:00:24,240 --> 00:00:25,520
temperature itself.

14
00:00:25,520 --> 00:00:27,600
It's the gap between the coffee and the

15
00:00:27,600 --> 00:00:30,000
room. Right now, that gap is sixty five

16
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:31,040
degrees.

17
00:00:31,040 --> 00:00:33,120
Newton's law of cooling, in its simplest

18
00:00:33,120 --> 00:00:34,960
form, says the coffee's temperature

19
00:00:34,960 --> 00:00:36,880
changes at a rate proportional to that

20
00:00:36,880 --> 00:00:39,280
gap. Twice the gap, twice the cooling

21
00:00:39,280 --> 00:00:41,600
speed. The rate of temperature change is

22
00:00:41,600 --> 00:00:43,680
minus k times the difference between the

23
00:00:43,680 --> 00:00:46,000
coffee temperature and room temperature.

24
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:48,640
Here, k is an effective cooling constant.

25
00:00:48,640 --> 00:00:50,720
It depends on the cup, the lid, the amount

26
00:00:50,720 --> 00:00:52,960
of liquid, and the surrounding air.

27
00:00:52,960 --> 00:00:55,600
The solution is an exponential: the gap

28
00:00:55,600 --> 00:00:57,520
shrinks by the same fraction in every

29
00:00:57,520 --> 00:00:59,200
equal stretch of time.

30
00:00:59,200 --> 00:01:01,280
T equals room temperature, plus the

31
00:01:01,280 --> 00:01:04,400
starting gap times e to the minus k t.

32
00:01:04,450 --> 00:01:05,970
For this example, let's assume the

33
00:01:05,970 --> 00:01:08,290
temperature gap halves every ten minutes.

34
00:01:08,290 --> 00:01:10,013
That corresponds to a k of about point

35
00:01:10,023 --> 00:01:11,810
zero seven per minute.

36
00:01:11,810 --> 00:01:15,170
So e to the minus k t is exactly one half,

37
00:01:15,170 --> 00:01:17,410
to the power t over ten.

38
00:01:17,410 --> 00:01:18,690
Watch the gap.

39
00:01:18,690 --> 00:01:20,690
It starts at sixty five degrees.

40
00:01:20,690 --> 00:01:22,530
After ten minutes, it's thirty two and a

41
00:01:22,530 --> 00:01:25,170
half. After twenty, about sixteen.

42
00:01:25,330 --> 00:01:27,170
After thirty, about eight.

43
00:01:27,170 --> 00:01:29,600
So the coffee goes from eighty five, to

44
00:01:29,610 --> 00:01:32,050
fifty two and a half, to about thirty six,

45
00:01:32,370 --> 00:01:33,890
to about twenty eight.

46
00:01:33,890 --> 00:01:35,970
It drops thirty two and a half degrees in

47
00:01:35,970 --> 00:01:37,810
the first ten minutes, and only about

48
00:01:37,810 --> 00:01:39,570
eight in the third ten.

49
00:01:39,570 --> 00:01:41,650
Fast, then slow.

50
00:01:41,650 --> 00:01:43,810
Here's the same story as a curve.

51
00:01:43,810 --> 00:01:45,650
The dashed line is the room.

52
00:01:45,650 --> 00:01:47,650
Look at the vertical gap between the curve

53
00:01:47,650 --> 00:01:48,610
and that line.

54
00:01:48,610 --> 00:01:50,930
At every ten minute mark, it's half what

55
00:01:50,930 --> 00:01:52,450
it was ten minutes earlier.

56
00:01:52,450 --> 00:01:54,370
The curve keeps getting closer to the room

57
00:01:54,370 --> 00:01:56,610
line, but in the model, it never quite

58
00:01:56,610 --> 00:01:57,890
reaches it.

59
00:01:57,890 --> 00:01:59,810
That's why the curve flattens.

60
00:01:59,810 --> 00:02:02,370
The cooling speed at any moment is k times

61
00:02:02,370 --> 00:02:04,440
the gap. At the very start, the gap is

62
00:02:04,450 --> 00:02:06,850
sixty five, so the coffee is cooling at

63
00:02:06,850 --> 00:02:08,850
about four and a half degrees per minute.

64
00:02:08,900 --> 00:02:11,060
At the ten minute mark, the gap is half as

65
00:02:11,060 --> 00:02:12,890
big, and so is the speed: about two point

66
00:02:12,900 --> 00:02:14,740
three degrees per minute.

67
00:02:14,740 --> 00:02:17,140
Smaller gap, gentler slope.

68
00:02:17,140 --> 00:02:18,900
So when does it reach a temperature you

69
00:02:18,900 --> 00:02:21,140
like? Say you want your coffee to cool to

70
00:02:21,140 --> 00:02:22,500
sixty degrees Celsius.

71
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:23,940
That's a gap of forty.

72
00:02:23,940 --> 00:02:26,580
So we need sixty five times one half, to

73
00:02:26,580 --> 00:02:29,460
the power t over ten, to equal forty.

74
00:02:29,460 --> 00:02:32,660
Take logs, and t equals ten times log base

75
00:02:32,820 --> 00:02:35,220
two of sixty five over forty.

76
00:02:35,220 --> 00:02:37,620
About seven minutes, for this mug and

77
00:02:37,620 --> 00:02:38,820
these conditions.

78
00:02:38,820 --> 00:02:40,500
Now the milk puzzle.

79
00:02:40,500 --> 00:02:42,020
You'll drink in ten minutes.

80
00:02:42,020 --> 00:02:44,500
Do you pour in cold milk now, or just

81
00:02:44,500 --> 00:02:45,700
before you drink?

82
00:02:45,940 --> 00:02:48,100
To compare fairly, we'll use the same

83
00:02:48,100 --> 00:02:50,260
coffee and the same milk both times.

84
00:02:50,260 --> 00:02:52,580
The milk stays at five degrees until it's

85
00:02:52,580 --> 00:02:54,740
poured, and it makes up one fifth of the

86
00:02:54,740 --> 00:02:57,300
final drink. We'll assume instant mixing,

87
00:02:57,300 --> 00:02:58,980
with the mixed temperature as a simple

88
00:02:58,980 --> 00:03:00,180
weighted average.

89
00:03:00,180 --> 00:03:02,420
And we'll assume both cups follow the same

90
00:03:02,420 --> 00:03:04,420
ten minute cooling half life.

91
00:03:04,770 --> 00:03:06,360
First, milk now.

92
00:03:06,370 --> 00:03:08,520
Point eight times eighty five, plus point

93
00:03:08,530 --> 00:03:11,490
two times five, gives sixty nine degrees.

94
00:03:11,490 --> 00:03:13,410
That's a gap of forty nine.

95
00:03:13,410 --> 00:03:15,560
After ten minutes, the gap halves to

96
00:03:15,570 --> 00:03:16,930
twenty four and a half.

97
00:03:16,930 --> 00:03:19,160
So at the ten minute mark, this cup is at

98
00:03:19,170 --> 00:03:21,170
forty four and a half degrees.

99
00:03:21,170 --> 00:03:22,770
Now milk later.

100
00:03:22,770 --> 00:03:24,690
The black coffee cools first.

101
00:03:24,690 --> 00:03:26,840
Its gap halves from sixty five to thirty

102
00:03:26,850 --> 00:03:29,480
two and a half, so at ten minutes it's at

103
00:03:29,490 --> 00:03:30,930
fifty two and a half.

104
00:03:30,930 --> 00:03:32,440
Then the cold milk goes in.

105
00:03:32,450 --> 00:03:34,530
Point eight times fifty two and a half,

106
00:03:34,610 --> 00:03:36,520
plus point two times five, gives forty

107
00:03:36,530 --> 00:03:37,970
three degrees.

108
00:03:37,970 --> 00:03:40,050
Compared at the same moment, right after

109
00:03:40,050 --> 00:03:42,290
the second cup gets its milk, the early

110
00:03:42,290 --> 00:03:45,090
milk cup is one and a half degrees warmer.

111
00:03:45,090 --> 00:03:46,370
Here's the picture.

112
00:03:46,370 --> 00:03:48,850
The blue cup drops at minute zero, when

113
00:03:48,850 --> 00:03:49,970
the milk goes in.

114
00:03:49,970 --> 00:03:52,450
The red cup drops at minute ten.

115
00:03:52,450 --> 00:03:54,770
Early milk reduces the temperature gap

116
00:03:54,770 --> 00:03:57,170
right away, so in this model, that cup's

117
00:03:57,170 --> 00:03:59,090
temperature falls more slowly during the

118
00:03:59,090 --> 00:04:01,650
wait. In this example, the model predicts

119
00:04:01,650 --> 00:04:03,890
a difference of one and a half degrees.

120
00:04:04,100 --> 00:04:06,020
In practice, adding milk changes the

121
00:04:06,020 --> 00:04:08,100
amount and makeup of the liquid, so the

122
00:04:08,100 --> 00:04:10,260
two cups can cool at different rates.

123
00:04:10,260 --> 00:04:12,500
And with the shared half life, if the milk

124
00:04:12,500 --> 00:04:14,500
started at room temperature, both cups

125
00:04:14,500 --> 00:04:16,260
would end at forty six degrees.

126
00:04:16,260 --> 00:04:18,100
Timing wouldn't matter.

127
00:04:18,100 --> 00:04:19,860
The same simple model shows up in

128
00:04:19,860 --> 00:04:22,100
engineering. A computer chip cooling after

129
00:04:22,100 --> 00:04:23,860
it shuts down behaves roughly this way,

130
00:04:23,940 --> 00:04:25,700
over a useful stretch of time.

131
00:04:25,700 --> 00:04:27,620
And a capacitor discharging through a

132
00:04:27,620 --> 00:04:30,180
fixed resistor follows it almost exactly.

133
00:04:30,180 --> 00:04:31,780
Wherever the rate of change is

134
00:04:31,780 --> 00:04:33,860
proportional to the remaining gap, with a

135
00:04:33,860 --> 00:04:35,940
constant of proportionality, you get the

136
00:04:35,940 --> 00:04:37,540
same exponential.

137
00:04:37,540 --> 00:04:39,540
Real coffee follows this model only

138
00:04:39,540 --> 00:04:40,500
approximately.

139
00:04:40,500 --> 00:04:42,420
Evaporation can add to the cooling of an

140
00:04:42,420 --> 00:04:44,740
open mug, and a lid generally slows

141
00:04:44,740 --> 00:04:47,060
cooling, partly by limiting evaporation.

142
00:04:47,060 --> 00:04:49,460
But in this model, each ten minute stretch

143
00:04:49,460 --> 00:04:51,220
cuts the remaining temperature gap in

144
00:04:51,220 --> 00:04:53,780
half: fast at first, then slow.

145
00:04:53,780 --> 00:04:56,020
And in our example, adding the cold milk

146
00:04:56,020 --> 00:04:57,780
